Change files: release intent, one per PR.

Every PR that changes a crate adds one here with cargo-rail:

    cargo rail change add <every crate you changed> \
      --bump <none|patch|minor|major> -m "<release note>"

and commits the .changes/<slug>.md it writes. CI (`cargo rail change
check`, in checks.yml's lint job) fails a PR whose changed crates have
none.

- The note is for the user: start with a verb (Added / Fixed / Improved /
  Changed / Removed / Security) and end with the app, `(signal)` or
  `(session)`.
- --bump none: nothing a user would notice (refactor, CI, tests, docs).
  It satisfies the check and adds no release-notes line.
- --bump major: a breaking change (a title with `!`, anything under
  migrations/). Add a `Migration:` paragraph to the file body: what
  changed, why, the exact steps.

- A change to STORED DATA (a schema, a storage format, a migration of a
  vault, an index or a database): add a line to the body,

      Data-Migration: <what changes, and whether a rollback undoes it>

  at any bump level. The release's notes list it first ("Data migration"),
  and its release-facts.json (signed with the release) says
  "data_migration": true: the server's release bump never deploys that
  release by itself, because rolling the binary back does not roll the
  data back.

`just release` (scripts/release.sh) builds a release's notes from the
files ADDED since the previous tag (major -> Breaking / Migration, minor
-> Added, patch -> Fixed and improved) and they go into the GitHub
Release. Nothing deletes them: they stay as the record. CHANGELOG.md
ends at 26.10.3 and is no longer written.

This file has no .md extension on purpose: cargo-rail reads every .md
here as a change file and rejects one without the front matter.

The fleet rules: the `release-workflow` skill (references/changelog.md).
